 "source_type": "instagram",
 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DgJyuGLOt1P/",
 "title": "How to Introduce Yourself as a Business Coach",
 "topic": "communication",
 "summary": "Dr. Forbes Riley demonstrates how to fix an elevator pitch for a business coach. Instead of using cute or vague answers, clearly state your role, highlight your years of experience, and state quantified monetary results before ending with an inviting question.",
 "key_points": [
  "Avoid vague or cute introductions that hide what you actually do, as it leaves prospects confused rather than interested in working with you.",
  "State your title clearly upfront (e.g., business coach) so listeners immediately understand your role.",
  "Highlight your total years in the field (e.g., over 40 years) to build immediate credibility.",
  "Quantify your total impact with concrete financial numbers (e.g., coached over $3 billion worth of business).",
  "Close the pitch with a compelling question like, 'Can you imagine what I might be able to do for you?'"
 ],
 "content": "Post text: The better you get at your work, the harder it becomes to explain it to someone outside your field. You pick up the jargon and see every nuance, so when someone asks, 'So, what do you do?' twenty years of expertise comes out as one long ramble. You can see their eyes glaze over, and you keep talking anyway because stopping feels even worse. I've been there. I used to list everything I'd ever done, from actress and TV host to fitness expert and speaker, and I watched people tune out every single time. Once I learned to say it in one line, everything changed, and now when I tell people I'm known as the Queen of Pitch, they lean in and ask me to tell them more. I'm Dr. Forbes Riley, and I've sold $2.5 billion on live TV across 197 infomercials, often in categories where I wasn't the expert. Imagine what the same formula can do for someone who is. If you're a financial advisor, therapist, chiropractor, attorney, nurse, consultant or coach who knows far more than you can say in nine seconds, this is for you. You aren't underqualified; you just haven't been given the right words yet, and that's something we can fix together. Comment YES and I'll DM you a free invite to my next live Pitch Secrets masterclass, and bring the pitch you've been stuck on for years. Remember, you don't get what you deserve, you get what you pitch for. Make a note of this post. Transcript: Dr. Forbes Riley: Somebody asks you what do you do, what do you say? Man: I work with successful people just like you. And we work on issues like what's next after you're successful? Dr. Forbes Riley: And what do you really do? Man: I'm a business coach. Dr. Forbes Riley: Okay, so one of the problems is because he didn't say that, my next question was not, can I work with you? It was like, wait a second, you never said business coach. It was very cute, but it wasn't quite right. So let me play it differently. And by the way, it's not one size fits all. How long have you been doing what you do? Man: 40 years. Dr. Forbes Riley: 40 years. If you had to add up all the successes and all the lives that you've changed to how much money it might have generated, what would you say? Man: $3 billion. Dr. Forbes Riley: Okay, so you had a nice little picture over there, but I wouldn't do it that way. I would say, as someone who's been a business coach for over 40 years—I know I don't look that old, but trust me, I've got experience—I've coached over $3 billion worth of business. Can you imagine what I might be able to do for you? Man: Yes, ma'am. Text on screen: How To Introduce Yourself As A Business Coach; WANT TO PERFECT YOUR PITCH? JOIN ME THIS SUNDAY, LIVE. Comment 'PITCH'",
